  "summary": "Redwood City Council is reviewing a San Mateo County Civil Grand Jury report critical of local ALPR policies and will host two public forums in October ahead of selecting a new license plate reader vendor. Seven vendors including Flock, Axon, and Motorola submitted proposals; the city notified Flock it will not auto-renew its contract expiring in November. A city audit found Redwood City ALPR data was improperly searched at least 99 times by four outside entities.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "city license plate reader data was improperly searched at least 99 times by four entities, namely Grand Oaks Homeowners Association, Flock Real-Time Crime Center, Veterans Affairs Loma Linda Healthcare police and Beverly Hills Unified School District police",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "the report called the city's ALPR policy \"outdated\" because it still described its operations as being mediated by the Northern California Regional Intelligence Center",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "The city has notified Flock that it will not automatically renew the agreement, though the company was eligible to submit a new proposal and did",
      "paragraph_idx": 19
    }
  ],
